description:
metadata.rs:102-107 — `metadata_changes` walks p.extra and on each iteration calls `args.as_object_mut().expect("args is constructed as an Object")` to obtain the inner Map, then `.entry(k).or_insert(v)`. The .as_object_mut().expect() pair is a loop invariant: `args` does not change shape across iterations.

Idiomatic Rust hoists invariant computation out of the loop body:
```rust
let obj = args.as_object_mut().expect("args is constructed as an Object");
for (k, v) in p.extra {
    obj.entry(k).or_insert(v);
}
```

What goes wrong if it stays: nothing at runtime (LLVM probably inlines / hoists the as_object_mut call, and `.expect()` is cheap on Some). The cost is *clarity*: each loop iteration as written reads as if it might re-evaluate the args shape, when in fact it cannot. A reader has to convince themselves that the per-iteration call is OK before moving on.

Note: this is NOT propagatable from canonical jmap-mail-client because mail-client's extras loop (email.rs:55-59) uses `args[k] = v` — a different (overwrite-on-collision) semantics with implicit per-iteration as_object_mut. Metadata-client deliberately uses entry+or_insert to preserve typed fields on collision (see JMAP-wzq9.18). The hoisting fix applies only here.

Scope: metadata.rs:102-107 (one site).
